Alien LT Wand V3 R1 — geometry and assembly guide
Units mm, g, gf. X points aft from the stylus, Y across the wand, Z upwards from LP level. Pitch axis X=237.1, Z=0. The self-contained STEP defines the exact freeform surfaces.

1. Preserve the C03 forebody and continue the descending crown beyond X=220. Form the closed left and right skins about Y=0, with a 38 mm wide aft body, a nominal 19 mm plan radius and a rounded lower end at X=305.
2. Centre the fixed vertical sleeve at X=286, Y=0. Mark Ø30 on both the sloping crown and sole using planar recesses 0.50 deep at centre. Withdraw the two marking inserts vertically before lateral mould separation. File to the actual sleeve and measured adhesive clearance after cure. Computer assembly uses Ø30.20 prepared holes.
3. Bond the 30/27 fixed sleeve and the two local internal carbon doublers into the shell. Fit the 25/23, 46 long counterweight cartridge in two size-matched guide rings. Use the curved shoe and radial screw for vertical locking.
4. Fit a bonded floor and two 0.8-thick vertical webs crossing at 90 degrees in the moving tube. Fill through the four open sectors from above, then fit gasket and removable carbon lid. Loose fill is represented in the mass budget rather than as individual STEP solids.
5. For each side, mark a horizontal tube axis from X=254 to 300 at Y=±23.1098 and Z=−4.8. Seat radius 5.08, contact angle 72 degrees, nominal radial bond gap 0.08. Post-bond a 10/8 stock tube per side, with internal thread and a 6 g axial travelling mass in each. Equal 9.88 mm displacements produce a 0.50 gf change at a 237.1 mm stylus lever arm.
6. Set coarse pitch balance with the sector fill. Estimate four equal portions from the model, then weigh the built assembly. Shift the loaded cartridge vertically and re-lock. The modeled design case is 1.75 gf stylus force and moving-group COM 0.50 mm below the LP/pivot plane.
7. Treat fastening, structural strength, physical cartridge sealing and full deck clearances as prototype checks before fabrication release. The STEP represents the wand candidate and counterweight hardware. Pickup, separate pivot mechanism and deck are outside this STEP.
